WEBVTT

00:00:00.975 --> 00:00:05.738
Welcome to Day 10, the last day of the course: Digital Presence and Social Media

00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:06.825
Management.

00:00:07.575 --> 00:00:11.425
We'll start with social media roles, then brand voice and style,

00:00:11.425 --> 00:00:13.562
and platforms and publishing.

00:00:13.562 --> 00:00:18.675
After that, we'll cover content, SEO and copy, then metrics and monitoring,

00:00:18.675 --> 00:00:22.275
and risk and compliance, and we'll close with the capstone.

00:00:23.025 --> 00:00:27.500
By the end of today, you'll be able to read social performance numbers correctly,

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:32.950
write for the platform, and understand the basic math behind a campaign's ROI.

00:00:34.875 --> 00:00:38.100
Section 1 of 7: Social Media Roles.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.088
We'll separate social media management from marketing,

00:00:41.088 --> 00:00:46.150
look at the EA and PA roles in social media, and keep personal and firm accounts

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:47.125
apart.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:54.138
Managing social media keeps an account alive; marketing brings new people in.

00:00:54.138 --> 00:00:58.250
People use the two words as if they mean the same thing, but they don't.

00:00:59.075 --> 00:01:02.775
Managing is about showing up consistently: regular posts,

00:01:02.775 --> 00:01:05.650
quick replies and a profile that's up to date.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.400
It never really stops.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.475
Marketing is about growth: a campaign with a goal,

00:01:10.475 --> 00:01:13.488
like clicks or sign-ups, and a start and end date.

00:01:13.488 --> 00:01:16.950
Mix them up, and you judge good work by the wrong numbers.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:23.575
First, before taking on any social media task, decide whether it's management or

00:01:23.575 --> 00:01:24.675
marketing.

00:01:24.675 --> 00:01:27.150
Then, use the right measure for each.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:30.600
Judge marketing by likes, and good work looks like failure.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.525
Finally, if a task covers both, split it into ongoing management and a separate

00:01:35.525 --> 00:01:36.700
campaign.

00:01:37.475 --> 00:01:39.725
Here's a situation to work through.

00:01:39.725 --> 00:01:44.512
At the quarterly review, Elias asks why his LinkedIn 'isn't bringing in clients'

00:01:44.512 --> 00:01:46.425
when you've posted every week.

00:01:46.425 --> 00:01:49.875
What were you measuring, and what should you measure for his question?

00:01:51.775 --> 00:01:56.938
On social media, the EA protects the brand and the PA produces the content.

00:01:56.938 --> 00:01:59.475
The two roles naturally divide.

00:02:00.225 --> 00:02:04.800
The EA side is strategy and protection: planning what goes out on professional

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.300
platforms, and spotting a PR problem early.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.575
The PA side is creation.

00:02:12.475 --> 00:02:17.200
As the EA, first, run the content calendar and time posts well.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.475
Next, filter messages and comments for real opportunities or urgent issues.

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:27.350
Then, check every post against the executive's image and company policy before it

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:28.600
goes live.

00:02:30.525 --> 00:02:35.612
After that, as the PA, create the content: capture real moments and edit them with

00:02:35.612 --> 00:02:37.325
tools like Canva.

00:02:37.325 --> 00:02:42.250
Finally, keep profiles and links current and reply to personal comments.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.638
Take a second with this one.

00:02:44.638 --> 00:02:50.212
A local newspaper criticizes the firm, and on the same day, Elias wins an award.

00:02:50.212 --> 00:02:54.475
Who handles each, the EA or the PA, and what goes out first?

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.938
Decide who owns each social media account before anyone leaves the firm, not after.

00:03:01.938 --> 00:03:04.200
Who controls the login matters.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:08.562
If an executive leaves and nobody agreed whether the account belonged to them or the

00:03:08.562 --> 00:03:11.100
firm, you've got a real dispute.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:16.025
First, write down which accounts are personal and which belong to the firm.

00:03:16.025 --> 00:03:21.350
Next, set up access to match: a firm account should have firm-controlled access.

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:23.763
Finally, give each its own voice.

00:03:23.763 --> 00:03:29.000
Personal ones can show more personality; firm ones stay closer to the firm's voice.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.312
Think about this one.

00:03:31.312 --> 00:03:35.375
A junior associate posts an office photo on his personal account,

00:03:35.375 --> 00:03:38.250
and a draft contract is visible on his screen.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:39.525
It's his account.

00:03:39.525 --> 00:03:41.000
Is it the firm's business?

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:42.500
What happens next?

00:03:44.425 --> 00:03:47.975
Section 2 of 7: Brand Voice and Style.

00:03:47.975 --> 00:03:51.013
We'll build an executive's personal brand style guide,

00:03:51.013 --> 00:03:54.975
pin down the brand voice, and keep the visual brand consistent.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:00.263
An executive's online voice has to be written down,

00:04:00.263 --> 00:04:03.000
or every new writer will guess it differently.

00:04:03.775 --> 00:04:06.425
A style guide writes down five things.

00:04:06.425 --> 00:04:11.062
The first is the north star: the main goal, and the three subjects the executive is

00:04:11.062 --> 00:04:12.450
the expert on.

00:04:14.375 --> 00:04:18.075
Then, the voice: first person or third, emojis or not.

00:04:18.075 --> 00:04:21.675
And the 'never' list: topics and words that are off limits.

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:28.050
Next, how to engage: who gets a reply, and what to do about trolls.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:32.075
And the look: mostly natural photos, with a few polished ones.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.850
To build one, first, define the north star.

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:39.750
Next, write down the voice and tone rules.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:44.175
Then, make the 'never' list specific, down to buzzwords and formatting.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:50.350
After that, set the engagement rules, including what needs approval.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:55.775
Finally, agree the visual standard, so photos look consistent whoever picks them.

00:04:56.525 --> 00:04:58.788
Here's a situation to work through.

00:04:58.788 --> 00:05:03.900
A new contractor writes a post for Elias criticizing a judge's recent ruling.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:08.088
It's clever and would get attention, and nothing in the guide says it's banned.

00:05:08.088 --> 00:05:09.700
What should the guide have said?

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:15.450
A useful way to pin down a voice is to place it on four scales:

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:20.325
funny or serious, formal or casual, calm or enthusiastic,

00:05:20.325 --> 00:05:22.475
and rebellious or respectful.

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:27.975
Once we've chosen a point on each, that's where the voice stays, whoever is writing.

00:05:27.975 --> 00:05:32.075
If it drifts, the audience notices, even if they can't say why.

00:05:34.025 --> 00:05:37.200
First, apply 'this, not that' boundaries: confident,

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.525
not sarcastic; witty, not arrogant; accessible,

00:05:40.525 --> 00:05:43.675
not simplistic; bold, not aggressive.

00:05:43.675 --> 00:05:48.588
Next, check new posts against your positions on the scales, not just gut feel.

00:05:48.588 --> 00:05:52.350
Then, if the voice wobbles, work out which scale it's drifting on.

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:56.450
Because a voice that changes every few days makes people unfollow.

00:05:57.225 --> 00:05:58.813
Take a second with this one.

00:05:58.813 --> 00:06:02.150
A post in Elias's voice gets strong engagement,

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:06.425
but a partner calls it 'too casual for a lawyer.' How do you decide whether it's

00:06:06.425 --> 00:06:09.050
on-voice, and what do you check it against?

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.475
Let's compare Elias's real voice with a generic one.

00:06:14.475 --> 00:06:18.888
His gets straight to the point, backs up claims with specifics and doesn't jump on

00:06:18.888 --> 00:06:20.200
every trend.

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:25.963
The generic version hedges with 'we believe', leans on buzzwords like 'innovative'

00:06:25.963 --> 00:06:29.213
and chases whatever format is trending this week.

00:06:29.213 --> 00:06:33.400
Side by side, it's obvious which one sounds worth listening to.

00:06:35.475 --> 00:06:38.800
So, first, lead with the actual point, not a warm-up.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:43.913
Next, back every claim with a specific detail, not an adjective like 'amazing'.

00:06:43.913 --> 00:06:45.875
Then, skip the trends.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:52.875
After that, find hedging phrases in drafts and rewrite them as direct statements.

00:06:52.875 --> 00:06:58.075
Finally, compare any draft with a real example of his voice before publishing.

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:01.063
Here's a situation to work through.

00:07:01.063 --> 00:07:03.825
The firm lost a big case in the news this week,

00:07:03.825 --> 00:07:08.088
and Elias's next scheduled post is a celebratory team photo.

00:07:08.088 --> 00:07:10.075
Same voice, but what tone now?

00:07:10.075 --> 00:07:11.650
Do you still post it?

00:07:13.575 --> 00:07:18.175
A style guide stops being followed within a month unless someone is named to check

00:07:18.175 --> 00:07:19.650
drafts against it.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:24.275
And adjectives like 'confident' mean nothing to a new writer.

00:07:24.275 --> 00:07:28.700
Real example sentences, showing what confident looks like and what it doesn't,

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:30.400
are what teach the voice.

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:36.050
First, fill the guide with 'this, not that' example sentences.

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:40.788
Next, add messaging rules: the claims and descriptions that must always stay the

00:07:40.788 --> 00:07:41.688
same.

00:07:41.688 --> 00:07:45.225
Then, check new writers' first few drafts closely.

00:07:45.225 --> 00:07:48.900
Finally, update the guide when a good new pattern appears.

00:07:49.675 --> 00:07:51.800
Here's a situation to work through.

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:55.025
The firm's description changes from post to post:

00:07:55.025 --> 00:07:59.975
'boutique litigation firm,' 'full-service firm,' 'employment specialists.' What

00:07:59.975 --> 00:08:03.525
messaging rule fixes this, and who decides the wording?

00:08:05.450 --> 00:08:09.625
A strong palette usually has just four colors, each with one job.

00:08:10.425 --> 00:08:14.638
A main color that carries the brand, like a deep navy for authority.

00:08:14.638 --> 00:08:18.863
An accent, like a warm gold, used sparingly to draw the eye.

00:08:18.863 --> 00:08:21.025
A text color, like charcoal.

00:08:21.025 --> 00:08:25.600
And a calm background, like warm ivory, that never competes with the rest.

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:31.963
First, hold it to those four roles, and keep the accent for highlights and calls to

00:08:31.963 --> 00:08:33.050
action.

00:08:33.050 --> 00:08:38.200
Then, apply the palette to everything, not just the pieces one person designs.

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:44.125
Finally, when you look at any brand, check it against these four roles.

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:47.163
Here's a situation to work through.

00:08:47.163 --> 00:08:50.500
The firm's logo is slightly different blues on the website,

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:53.188
the business cards and the email signature.

00:08:53.188 --> 00:08:56.100
What's missing, and how do you fix it everywhere?

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:01.963
Restraint is what makes a brand look deliberate instead of random.

00:09:01.963 --> 00:09:05.525
The same rule applies to colors, fonts and images:

00:09:05.525 --> 00:09:09.250
choose a few, decide the rules in advance and stick to them.

00:09:10.025 --> 00:09:14.625
First, keep the four-color discipline and resist a 'just in case' fifth color.

00:09:14.625 --> 00:09:19.250
Next, pick one heading font and one body font, and use them everywhere.

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:23.825
Then, set clear image rules, like no cheesy stock photos.

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:29.888
After that, review recent content now and then for drift.

00:09:29.888 --> 00:09:34.775
Mixing fonts and colors across posts is one of the fastest ways to make a brand look

00:09:34.775 --> 00:09:35.975
unmanaged.

00:09:36.725 --> 00:09:38.425
Take a second with this one.

00:09:38.425 --> 00:09:42.825
Three recent posts for the firm used three different fonts and a stock photo of a

00:09:42.825 --> 00:09:43.813
gavel.

00:09:43.813 --> 00:09:47.025
What three rules would you add to stop it happening again?

00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:52.650
Section 3 of 7: Platforms and Publishing.

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:57.225
We'll cover platform-specific best practices, the content calendar and publishing

00:09:57.225 --> 00:10:00.625
workflow, and video and accessibility basics.

00:10:02.525 --> 00:10:06.525
Being 'good at social media' is really several separate skills.

00:10:07.325 --> 00:10:12.250
Each social platform has its own format, so pasting the same text everywhere rarely

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:13.300
works.

00:10:13.300 --> 00:10:18.138
Website tools, like WordPress or Squarespace, need enough confidence to update a

00:10:18.138 --> 00:10:19.950
page without breaking it.

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:24.475
And newsletter tools, like Mailchimp, need us to split lists sensibly and read the

00:10:24.475 --> 00:10:25.600
results.

00:10:27.525 --> 00:10:30.500
So, first, adapt content to each platform.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:35.150
Next, learn the firm's website tool well enough to make simple updates safely.

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:40.600
Then, learn the newsletter tool's basics: segments, send times and reports.

00:10:42.525 --> 00:10:47.000
After that, don't assume skill on one platform carries over to another.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:52.075
Finally, walk through any new tool's publishing steps before using it live.

00:10:52.825 --> 00:10:54.575
Take a second with this one.

00:10:54.575 --> 00:11:00.088
The firm's newsletter goes to 1,800 people, but only 9 percent open it.

00:11:00.088 --> 00:11:03.725
What would you look at in the newsletter tool, and what's one change you'd test

00:11:03.725 --> 00:11:04.650
first?

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:09.075
Each platform rewards something different.

00:11:09.075 --> 00:11:12.388
LinkedIn rewards a clear point in the first two lines.

00:11:12.388 --> 00:11:16.963
Instagram rewards strong visuals, with the caption in a supporting role.

00:11:16.963 --> 00:11:19.400
X rewards short and timely.

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:24.613
But one rule applies everywhere: test in draft or preview first.

00:11:24.613 --> 00:11:29.325
A typo in a draft is invisible; a typo sent to a real list isn't.

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:35.100
On the website, make routine updates through the visual editor.

00:11:35.875 --> 00:11:38.000
Here's a situation to work through.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:42.262
You're sending the monthly client newsletter, and the preview shows an old event

00:11:42.262 --> 00:11:43.713
date in the footer.

00:11:43.713 --> 00:11:46.475
The send is scheduled for 10 minutes' time.

00:11:46.475 --> 00:11:48.225
What do you do, in order?

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:54.138
Posting every day on the fly is exhausting, and it shows.

00:11:54.138 --> 00:11:58.875
A calmer way is to plan a month at a time, with each week given one job.

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:04.875
First, week one is ideas: brainstorm 12 to 15 from the content pillars.

00:12:04.875 --> 00:12:10.463
Next, week two is creation: film and design everything in one or two focused days.

00:12:10.463 --> 00:12:15.175
Then, week three is polish: pick the caption that best fits the voice.

00:12:15.175 --> 00:12:20.000
Finally, week four is scheduling: load everything into the publishing tool.

00:12:20.775 --> 00:12:23.038
Here's a situation to work through.

00:12:23.038 --> 00:12:26.650
Elias is in trial for two weeks and can't review anything,

00:12:26.650 --> 00:12:28.912
and ten posts need his sign-off.

00:12:28.912 --> 00:12:32.225
What should have happened a week earlier, and what do you do now?

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:39.888
Video shows an executive's real presence, including tone, pace and body language.

00:12:39.888 --> 00:12:42.525
That's a big opportunity and a real risk.

00:12:42.525 --> 00:12:47.025
And a short video with one clear point beats a polished one that rambles.

00:12:47.825 --> 00:12:51.950
First, before recording, agree the single point the video needs to make.

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:54.738
Next, check the background and the sound.

00:12:54.738 --> 00:12:57.300
Anything visible in the frame is public.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:01.950
Finally, keep social videos short, about 60 to 90 seconds.

00:13:02.725 --> 00:13:04.938
Here's a situation to work through.

00:13:04.938 --> 00:13:09.388
A video goes live, and a viewer comments that Elias's phone screen,

00:13:09.388 --> 00:13:13.125
visible for two seconds, shows a client's text message.

00:13:13.125 --> 00:13:15.700
What do you do immediately, and who do you tell?

00:13:17.625 --> 00:13:22.138
Alt text, captions and readable color contrast aren't nice extras.

00:13:22.138 --> 00:13:26.450
They decide whether a real share of your audience can use the content at all.

00:13:26.450 --> 00:13:29.613
Built in from the start, they cost almost nothing;

00:13:29.613 --> 00:13:32.875
going back to fix a year of posts costs a lot.

00:13:33.675 --> 00:13:38.875
First, write alt text for every image: what's actually shown and why it matters.

00:13:38.875 --> 00:13:42.950
Next, caption every video, and check the automatic captions.

00:13:42.950 --> 00:13:47.325
Finally, check color contrast on graphics and text over images.

00:13:48.075 --> 00:13:49.813
Take a second with this one.

00:13:49.813 --> 00:13:55.050
Elias's automatic captions turn 'arbitration' into 'our bitter nation,' and the

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:57.450
video's had 300 views.

00:13:57.450 --> 00:14:01.025
What do you do, and what step goes into the approval process?

00:14:02.950 --> 00:14:06.975
Section 4 of 7: Content, SEO and Copy.

00:14:06.975 --> 00:14:09.550
We'll look at audience psychology and pain points,

00:14:09.550 --> 00:14:15.500
SEO, GEO and funneling, and the difference between copywriting and blog writing.

00:14:17.425 --> 00:14:22.350
Good content names a real worry your audience has and answers it honestly.

00:14:23.125 --> 00:14:24.388
There are four to know.

00:14:24.388 --> 00:14:26.350
Money worries, like hidden fees.

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:27.988
Information overload.

00:14:27.988 --> 00:14:28.825
Burnout.

00:14:28.825 --> 00:14:32.200
And trust worries, the fear that what you're seeing is staged.

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:37.325
First, for money worries, be openly transparent.

00:14:37.325 --> 00:14:42.575
Next, for information overload, be the one who answers a single question quickly.

00:14:42.575 --> 00:14:46.850
Then, for burnout, talk about sustainable progress, not hustle.

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:52.700
After that, for trust worries, show the real process behind the scenes,

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:54.525
not staged content.

00:14:54.525 --> 00:14:58.550
Finally, use persuasion techniques ethically and sparingly.

00:14:59.325 --> 00:15:01.513
Here's a situation to work through.

00:15:01.513 --> 00:15:04.975
A partner wants a post that says 'Hundreds of businesses trust us,

00:15:04.975 --> 00:15:07.450
so should you!' with a stock photo.

00:15:07.450 --> 00:15:11.200
What makes this weak, and what real proof could you use instead?

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:16.413
There are three ways people find an executive online.

00:15:16.413 --> 00:15:19.750
Search engines, where SEO helps them show up.

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:24.950
And AI tools, where GEO helps them get recommended when someone asks a relevant

00:15:24.950 --> 00:15:26.050
question.

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:32.075
The third is the funnel, which moves someone from first hearing about them,

00:15:32.075 --> 00:15:35.338
to being interested, to actually getting in touch.

00:15:35.338 --> 00:15:38.825
A simple monthly scorecard shows whether it's all working.

00:15:40.775 --> 00:15:44.900
First, for search, use the natural phrases the audience actually types,

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:47.900
and earn links from podcasts and guest articles.

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:52.025
Next, for AI tools, structure content so they can quote it.

00:15:52.025 --> 00:15:55.325
Then, for the funnel, match content to the stage:

00:15:55.325 --> 00:16:00.350
social posts for awareness, newsletters for interest and a booking link for action.

00:16:02.275 --> 00:16:04.825
After that, track the monthly scorecard.

00:16:04.825 --> 00:16:08.675
Finally, search the executive's name privately every month.

00:16:09.450 --> 00:16:11.713
Here's a situation to work through.

00:16:11.713 --> 00:16:17.025
Elias's LinkedIn posts reach lots of people, but nobody books a consultation.

00:16:17.025 --> 00:16:20.525
Which stage of the funnel is missing, and what would you add?

00:16:22.475 --> 00:16:27.663
To be recommended by AI tools, the executive's bio needs to read exactly the same

00:16:27.663 --> 00:16:28.875
everywhere.

00:16:28.875 --> 00:16:34.350
AI assistants favor clear, well-structured, factual content from sources that agree

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:35.538
with each other.

00:16:35.538 --> 00:16:39.175
Even small differences between bios lower that trust.

00:16:39.975 --> 00:16:43.175
First, add structured data where the platform allows it,

00:16:43.175 --> 00:16:45.550
so machines can read the content.

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:49.800
Next, write clear question-and-answer content for common questions.

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:55.225
Then, keep bio wording identical on LinkedIn, the website and speaker pages.

00:16:57.150 --> 00:17:00.250
After that, check those bios regularly for drift,

00:17:00.250 --> 00:17:03.550
and make sure every new profile uses the same wording.

00:17:04.325 --> 00:17:06.463
Here's a situation to work through.

00:17:06.463 --> 00:17:11.900
An AI assistant describes Elias as a 'criminal defense lawyer.' He's never practiced

00:17:11.900 --> 00:17:13.000
criminal law.

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:16.075
What do you do about it, and which sources would you check?

00:17:18.025 --> 00:17:22.525
Copywriting closes the sale; blog writing educates and builds trust.

00:17:23.300 --> 00:17:27.238
Copy is short and built to get someone to act: grab attention,

00:17:27.238 --> 00:17:30.913
spark interest, build desire, then ask for action.

00:17:30.913 --> 00:17:34.688
A blog is longer and built to teach and to be found in search:

00:17:34.688 --> 00:17:37.675
a hook in the headline, a direct answer early on,

00:17:37.675 --> 00:17:40.125
and sections that are easy to skim.

00:17:42.075 --> 00:17:45.175
First, for copy, know the exact action you want.

00:17:45.175 --> 00:17:50.613
Next, write calls to action with strong verbs and real urgency, not 'click here'.

00:17:50.613 --> 00:17:55.750
Then, for blogs, open with the hook and the direct answer, and use clear headings.

00:17:57.675 --> 00:18:02.675
After that, add something first-hand, like a real example or unique data point.

00:18:02.675 --> 00:18:05.800
Finally, choose the format that fits the goal.

00:18:06.575 --> 00:18:08.850
Here's a situation to work through.

00:18:08.850 --> 00:18:13.813
Your blog post on 'how to prepare for a deposition' says exactly what the top five

00:18:13.813 --> 00:18:15.888
search results already say.

00:18:15.888 --> 00:18:20.125
What could you add from Elias's real experience to make it worth reading?

00:18:22.050 --> 00:18:25.475
Section 5 of 7: Metrics and Monitoring.

00:18:25.475 --> 00:18:29.975
We'll read engagement rate and its benchmarks, work the basic campaign math,

00:18:29.975 --> 00:18:31.925
and set up social listening.

00:18:33.875 --> 00:18:38.825
Engagement rate tells you how much people actually cared, not just how many saw it.

00:18:39.625 --> 00:18:43.825
Likes on their own can be misleading, because a big account will always get more

00:18:43.825 --> 00:18:45.288
than a small one.

00:18:45.288 --> 00:18:49.538
Engagement rate lets us compare a small account fairly with a big one,

00:18:49.538 --> 00:18:52.250
or this month's post with last month's.

00:18:54.175 --> 00:18:55.025
Here's how.

00:18:55.025 --> 00:18:58.175
First, add up the likes, comments and shares.

00:18:58.175 --> 00:19:02.225
Next, divide by the number of followers and multiply by 100.

00:19:02.225 --> 00:19:05.750
Then, compare posts by rate, not by raw likes.

00:19:07.675 --> 00:19:10.100
After that, recalculate regularly.

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:13.825
Finally, check your number against the platform's own figures.

00:19:14.575 --> 00:19:16.313
Take a second with this one.

00:19:16.313 --> 00:19:23.913
A post got 85 likes, 22 comments and 6 shares, and the account has 2,500 followers.

00:19:23.913 --> 00:19:28.450
What's the engagement rate, and is it good if the usual rate is 3 percent?

00:19:30.350 --> 00:19:31.175
Quick check.

00:19:31.175 --> 00:19:37.313
A post gets 200 likes, 20 comments and 30 shares on an account with 5,000 followers.

00:19:37.313 --> 00:19:38.900
What's the engagement rate?

00:19:38.900 --> 00:19:44.900
Is it A, 50 percent, B, 5 percent, C, 2 percent, or D, 10 percent?

00:19:45.725 --> 00:19:47.000
Take a second.

00:19:47.775 --> 00:19:50.363
The answer is B, 5 percent.

00:19:50.363 --> 00:19:53.763
Two hundred plus twenty plus thirty is 250.

00:19:53.763 --> 00:19:58.925
Divide by 5,000 and multiply by 100, and you get 5 percent.

00:20:00.850 --> 00:20:05.300
A small account with a high engagement rate can outperform a big one with a low

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:06.200
rate.

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:11.125
First, compare engagement rates, not follower or like counts.

00:20:11.125 --> 00:20:16.488
Next, on LinkedIn, 2 to 5 percent is healthy, and above 7 percent means a post is

00:20:16.488 --> 00:20:18.450
really taking off in its niche.

00:20:18.450 --> 00:20:21.475
Then, read reach and engagement together.

00:20:21.475 --> 00:20:25.825
High reach with low engagement means the hook or the audience is off.

00:20:27.750 --> 00:20:31.388
After that, treat low reach with high engagement as a good sign:

00:20:31.388 --> 00:20:33.625
a small, loyal audience.

00:20:33.625 --> 00:20:37.850
Finally, revisit the benchmarks, because platforms keep changing.

00:20:38.600 --> 00:20:40.263
Take a second with this one.

00:20:40.263 --> 00:20:45.738
Elias's post reached 15,000 people at 0.4 percent engagement.

00:20:45.738 --> 00:20:49.413
A smaller post reached 900 people at 8 percent.

00:20:49.413 --> 00:20:53.400
Which one tells you more about his audience, and what would you do next?

00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:59.600
Before calling a campaign a success, work out what each lead cost and what it earned

00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:00.500
back.

00:21:01.275 --> 00:21:04.263
Two simple sums tell us whether a campaign worked:

00:21:04.263 --> 00:21:07.225
cost per lead and return on investment.

00:21:07.225 --> 00:21:08.225
Why both?

00:21:08.225 --> 00:21:12.725
Because a huge number of leads can hide a campaign that actually lost money.

00:21:14.675 --> 00:21:19.425
First, work out cost per lead: total spend divided by the number of leads.

00:21:19.425 --> 00:21:23.375
Next, work out return on investment: revenue minus spend,

00:21:23.375 --> 00:21:26.200
divided by spend, times 100.

00:21:26.200 --> 00:21:28.275
Then, look at both together.

00:21:30.200 --> 00:21:32.975
After that, compare them with the campaign's goals,

00:21:32.975 --> 00:21:35.600
and track them across campaigns over time.

00:21:36.375 --> 00:21:38.563
Here's a situation to work through.

00:21:38.563 --> 00:21:45.513
A $3,000 campaign's 150 leads turned into 2 clients worth $1,000 each.

00:21:45.513 --> 00:21:47.875
What's the cost per lead and the return?

00:21:47.875 --> 00:21:50.550
What would you tell Elias about running it again?

00:21:52.450 --> 00:21:53.275
Quick check.

00:21:53.275 --> 00:21:57.025
A campaign spends $300 and generates 30 leads.

00:21:57.025 --> 00:21:58.475
What's the cost per lead?

00:21:58.475 --> 00:22:04.825
Is it A, $10, B, $30, C, $300, or D, $3?

00:22:05.650 --> 00:22:06.925
Take a second.

00:22:07.700 --> 00:22:14.425
The answer is A, $10. $300 divided by 30 leads is $10 per lead.

00:22:16.375 --> 00:22:20.563
Most of what people say about the firm online never tags the firm.

00:22:20.563 --> 00:22:24.300
Social listening means actively tracking those conversations,

00:22:24.300 --> 00:22:27.013
not just reading your own notifications.

00:22:27.013 --> 00:22:30.450
Catch something early, and you have far more options.

00:22:31.250 --> 00:22:35.775
First, set up alerts or regular checks for the executive's and firm's names.

00:22:35.775 --> 00:22:39.738
Next, separate routine mentions from ones that need attention.

00:22:39.738 --> 00:22:41.125
Most need nothing.

00:22:41.125 --> 00:22:45.400
Finally, log recurring themes, not just single mentions.

00:22:46.175 --> 00:22:48.313
Here's a situation to work through.

00:22:48.313 --> 00:22:52.813
Over three weeks, five different people post complaints about slow replies from the

00:22:52.813 --> 00:22:53.688
firm.

00:22:53.688 --> 00:22:55.225
None are dramatic.

00:22:55.225 --> 00:22:57.875
What do you do with the pattern, and who do you tell?

00:22:59.800 --> 00:23:03.175
Section 6 of 7: Risk and Compliance.

00:23:03.175 --> 00:23:07.625
We'll cover crisis response on social media, copyright and image rights,

00:23:07.625 --> 00:23:10.850
and the disclosure and legal advertising rules.

00:23:12.750 --> 00:23:17.050
A pile-on moves fast; you often have hours, not days.

00:23:17.050 --> 00:23:20.300
The instinct to delete or ignore is usually wrong,

00:23:20.300 --> 00:23:24.200
because a calm, visible response reads better than silence.

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:27.825
But not every negative comment deserves a public reply.

00:23:28.625 --> 00:23:34.475
First, assess before reacting: a real complaint, a misunderstanding or bad faith?

00:23:34.475 --> 00:23:38.050
Next, reply publicly with a short, calm acknowledgment,

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:40.675
and move the details to a private channel.

00:23:40.675 --> 00:23:45.000
Finally, bring in the executive or the right decision-maker for anything beyond

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:46.125
routine.

00:23:46.900 --> 00:23:49.000
Here's a situation to work through.

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:53.100
Your colleague has already replied to a complaint with 'This is completely false and

00:23:53.100 --> 00:23:55.550
you know it.' It's getting shared.

00:23:55.550 --> 00:23:58.150
What happens next, and who do you bring in?

00:24:00.100 --> 00:24:04.650
One borrowed photo can bring a legal claim, and for a law firm that's an especially

00:24:04.650 --> 00:24:05.800
bad look.

00:24:06.575 --> 00:24:09.775
Almost everything online is protected by copyright,

00:24:09.775 --> 00:24:13.150
and being able to download it doesn't mean the firm can post it.

00:24:13.150 --> 00:24:17.650
We use content the firm owns, content with a license that covers our use,

00:24:17.650 --> 00:24:20.338
or content we have written permission for.

00:24:20.338 --> 00:24:23.700
People in photos need to agree to appear in promotion.

00:24:23.700 --> 00:24:28.188
And for clients, that has to be in writing, because even showing that someone is a

00:24:28.188 --> 00:24:30.925
client can breach confidentiality.

00:24:32.850 --> 00:24:35.663
First, we source images from the firm's library,

00:24:35.663 --> 00:24:40.025
licensed stock or our own photographer, and check the license terms.

00:24:40.025 --> 00:24:43.788
Next, we keep a record for each image: source, license,

00:24:43.788 --> 00:24:45.988
date and any required credit.

00:24:45.988 --> 00:24:49.600
Then we get signed photo releases from staff and guests.

00:24:51.525 --> 00:24:54.725
After that, we use licensed music for videos.

00:24:54.725 --> 00:24:58.663
Finally, we share others' posts with the share button or with permission,

00:24:58.663 --> 00:25:00.450
never by re-uploading.

00:25:01.225 --> 00:25:03.488
Here's a situation to work through.

00:25:03.488 --> 00:25:07.713
A partner wants to post a great photo from last night's charity dinner:

00:25:07.713 --> 00:25:10.638
Elias with two clients and a local news anchor,

00:25:10.638 --> 00:25:13.175
taken by a guest who emailed it over.

00:25:13.175 --> 00:25:15.100
What do you need before it goes up?

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:21.588
When a partner, employee or influencer promotes the firm because of a relationship

00:25:21.588 --> 00:25:27.838
or a perk, disclosure rules can apply, like the US FTC's endorsement guidelines.

00:25:27.838 --> 00:25:31.538
People should be able to tell when a post exists because of a deal,

00:25:31.538 --> 00:25:33.975
not just genuine enthusiasm.

00:25:33.975 --> 00:25:38.775
And because the details change, confirm with firm policy or counsel.

00:25:39.575 --> 00:25:43.150
First, before agreeing any paid or reciprocal arrangement,

00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:47.050
confirm what disclosure is needed and where it has to appear.

00:25:47.050 --> 00:25:50.425
Next, keep records of every such relationship.

00:25:50.425 --> 00:25:55.213
Finally, when partners post about the firm, check the disclosure is really there and

00:25:55.213 --> 00:25:56.550
easy to see.

00:25:57.325 --> 00:25:59.388
Here's a situation to work through.

00:25:59.388 --> 00:26:03.788
A partner's friend posts 'Best lawyers in town!' about the firm.

00:26:03.788 --> 00:26:07.750
You later learn the partner asked him to, in exchange for a dinner.

00:26:07.750 --> 00:26:08.925
Is that a problem?

00:26:08.925 --> 00:26:10.050
What do you do?

00:26:12.025 --> 00:26:17.600
A law firm's social media is regulated advertising, not ordinary marketing.

00:26:17.600 --> 00:26:23.000
Most places have professional rules for how lawyers advertise, including online.

00:26:23.000 --> 00:26:27.800
And general education is fine, but a post that reads as advice for someone's

00:26:27.800 --> 00:26:31.500
specific situation crosses into practicing law.

00:26:32.300 --> 00:26:37.000
First, anything on a legal topic that reads as advice for a specific person needs

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:38.525
attorney review.

00:26:38.525 --> 00:26:44.450
Next, know the firm's local advertising rules, including any required disclaimers.

00:26:44.450 --> 00:26:48.225
Finally, when in doubt, escalate instead of publishing.

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:51.088
Here's a situation to work through.

00:26:51.088 --> 00:26:57.275
A happy client offers a video testimonial saying 'Elias won me $500,000 and he'll

00:26:57.275 --> 00:27:00.550
win for you too.' Can the firm post it as is?

00:27:00.550 --> 00:27:02.100
What do you check first?

00:27:04.025 --> 00:27:06.825
Section 7 of 7: Capstone.

00:27:06.825 --> 00:27:12.050
This is the final timed evaluation and the capstone checklist that close the course.

00:27:13.975 --> 00:27:16.025
This closes all ten days.

00:27:16.025 --> 00:27:20.250
The capstone isn't about getting through it; it's an honest look at what's solid and

00:27:20.250 --> 00:27:21.850
what still needs work.

00:27:22.650 --> 00:27:26.088
First, look at your actual results across all ten days:

00:27:26.088 --> 00:27:29.350
knowledge checks, practice labs and roleplays.

00:27:29.350 --> 00:27:33.675
Next, name the specific days or tools that still feel shaky.

00:27:33.675 --> 00:27:39.075
Finally, take the final evaluation with the same calm you've practiced all program.

00:27:39.825 --> 00:27:41.250
And one last thing.

00:27:41.250 --> 00:27:43.513
You start with Elias next Monday.

00:27:43.513 --> 00:27:46.800
Pause the video and write your 30-day practice plan:

00:27:46.800 --> 00:27:50.700
the two skills you'll keep working on and how you'll know you've improved.

00:27:52.675 --> 00:27:54.163
Now it's your turn.

00:27:54.163 --> 00:27:57.800
In today's Practice Lab, the Social Media Metrics Lab,

00:27:57.800 --> 00:28:01.788
you'll calculate real engagement rates to find the top-performing post,

00:28:01.788 --> 00:28:07.175
match tone to platform, and work the campaign math on cost-per-lead and ROI.

